~ Lava slice 14 — Screen: a write-only origin you render pixels to.
~ Drawing is a write, so an action that draws declares `writes Screen` exactly as
~ a printing one declares `writes Console`. "Which actions can draw?" stays a
~ header grep, like every other capability in the language.

create screen of size 16 by 16

~ the frame starts black; lay a background down first
fill screen with rgb 20, 20, 28

~ a gradient. Colour channels are EXPRESSIONS, so the pixel's colour is computed
~ from the cursors — which is the whole point of a pixel screen over a palette.
loop (create container "y" of type int with value 0) until (y is 16)
  loop (create container "x" of type int with value 0) until (x is 16)
    set pixel x, y to rgb [x * 17], [y * 17], 128
    update x to [x + 1]
  end
  update y to [y + 1]
end

~ the same drawing, but sealed in an action: the footprint has to admit Screen
create container "I" of type int with value 0

create action "DrawBorderPixel" reads I writes Screen as
  set pixel I, 0 to white
  set pixel I, 15 to white
  set pixel 0, I to white
  set pixel 15, I to white
end

loop 16 times
  DrawBorderPixel()
  update I to [I + 1]
end

~ `show` presents the frame. A program that drew but never called it still gets
~ one frame flushed at exit; an animation needs `show` once per frame.
show
print("rendered 16 by 16")
